{"authored" [{"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/state.json" "file_sha256" "53fd71314705cc27c121d2e590ddcf2bf95e3f37fe26ee975b9f6452012f9fd1" "basis" 1867 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacad