nd{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-4A+I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-4A+I) & =O \\\\ A-4I+{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =4I-A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\n$$\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "997af9925dcdd2b59bcf38208de40578874f274d8f29a005db34a7ddb748aeca"} {"question" "q-37778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "997af9925dcdd2b59bcf38208de40578874f274d8f29a005db34a7ddb748aeca"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{7}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3$, find $f(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}14-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(14-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )+192 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{10}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{12}$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,4x$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(2x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{10}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =2A-3I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "997af9925dcdd2b59bcf38208de40578874f274d8f29a005db34a7ddb748aeca"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -24 \\\\ 8 & -16\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -16\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}16 & -25 \\\\ 18 & -42\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}40 & 24 \\\\ -70 & -44\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-37844" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-8$ and $\\text{det}(A)=12.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-8A-12I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "997af9925dcdd2b59bcf38208de40578874f274d8f29a005db34a7ddb748aeca"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-8A+12I"] ["math" "-8A-12I"] ["math" "12A+8I"] ["math" "12A-8I"] ["math" "8A-12I"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072784/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0c065e0e0a42e6f765f4a33f2e157a509c54e1e061fef86c71f9f87d61df0e7"} {"question" "q-38702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8A-12I"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference